what is unix operating system?
  -As compared to windows os highly secured and data protection.
  -Unix is an operating system. 
  -The kernel is the base of the operating system.
  -it act as the bridge between the hardware and software.
  -it is a command line user interface os.
  -The kernel is responsible for managing critical tasks such as memory management, file systems, process control, and device communication.

disadvantage
   -complex design
   -os is not user friendly.
   

key features of UNIX
 -Open source and Free-> allows users to view, modify and distribute its source code freely.

 -Multiuser and multitasking->They allow several users to access system resources simultaneously and run multiple tasks concurrently without interference.

 -portability->it can run on any hardware architectures due to their implementation in C.

 -hierarchical File system->file system that organizes files and directories under a single root directory. This structure simplifies file management and access
 - Command-Line Interface (CLI)->enabling users to execute commands, automate tasks, and manage systems efficiently. This feature is highly valued by developers and system administrators.

Commands of Unix------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  
File and directory Management---
->ls- List files and directories
   -> -l>list show permissions,owner,size,date
   -> -a>includes hidden file(starting with(.))
   -> -h>Human readable file sizs(used with(-l))
   -> -R>Lists subdirectories
   -> -t>Sorts by modification time
   -> -S>Sorts by file size

->cd-change directory
   ->
   ->cd .. ->Moves up one level to the parent directory
   ->cd /  ->Goes to the root directory
   ->cd ~  ->Goes to your home directory
   ->cd -  ->Switches to the previous directory you were in
   ->cd /path/to/folder	->Goes directly to a specific folder

->pwd- this command tells that exactly where your in the file hierarchy or it displays the absolute path of the directory

->mkdir -creates the new directory

   ->mkdir folder_name     ->Creates the named folder
   ->mkdir folder2 folder3 -> Creeates multiple folders
   ->mkdir -p a/b/c        ->Creates nested folders,including parents
   ->mkdir -p existing_folder ->Avoids error if folder already exists

->rmdir - delete the empty directory

     ->rmdir folder ->Removes an empty folder
     ->rmdir --ignore-fail-on-non-empty folder->Suppresses error if folder isn't empty
     ->rmdir --parents a/b/c ->Removes nested empty folders up the path

->rm -remove the files or directory

    ->rm file.txt	>Deletes a file
    ->rm -r folder	>Deletes a folder and its contents
    ->rm -f file.txt	>Force deletes a file without confirmation
    ->rm -rf folder	>Force deletes a folder and everything inside

->cp -(copy)-it allows you to duplicate the files and directories from one location to another
      
     ->cp file1.txt file2.txt	        >Copies a file
     ->cp file.txt /path/	        >Copies file to another directory
     ->cp -r folder1 folder2	        >Copies a directory and its contents
     ->cp -i file.txt /path/	        >Prompts before overwriting
     ->cp -u file.txt /path/	        >Copies only if source is newer
     ->cp -v file.txt /path/	        >Shows progress of copying

->mv -(move) -move or rename the files
       
      ->mv old.txt new.txt	>Renames a file
      ->mv file.txt /path/	>Moves file to another directory
      ->mv folder/ /path/	>Moves a folder
      ->mv -i file.txt /path/	>Prompts before overwriting
      ->mv -v file.txt /path/	>Shows progress of move


->touch -to create the new file or update the time stamp
      
        ->touch file.txt	                Creates an empty file or updates timestamp
        ->touch file1 file2	                Creates multiple files
        ->touch -c file.txt	                Updates timestamp only if file exists
        ->touch -t 202510091230 file.txt	Sets a custom timestamp

---------->File viewing commands<----------------------------------------

->cat-concatenate- View the contents of a file, Combine multiple files into one.
      
->cat file.txt	                        >Displays file content                                            
->cat file1 file2	                >Displays multiple files
->cat > newfile.txt	                >Creates a new file with typed input
->cat file1 file2 > combined.txt	>Combines files into one
->cat -n file.txt	                >Shows file with line numbers
       

-n	Number all output lines
-b	Number non-blank lines only
-s	Suppress repeated empty lines
-E	Show $ at end of each line
-T	Show tabs as ^I


->more-The more command lets you read the contents of a file page by page. 
       It pauses after each screenful of text, allowing you to scroll through the file interactively.

->less is a file pager — it lets you view the contents of a file one screen at a time, just like more, but with extra features:

     You can scroll backward and forward

     You can search within the file

     It’s non-destructive — it doesn’t modify the file

->head-The head command displays the first few lines of a file. By default, it shows the first 10 lines, but you can customize this using options.
     
      ->head file.txt	        >Shows first 10 lines of a file
      ->head -n 5 file.txt	>Shows first 5 lines
      ->head -c 100 file.txt	>Shows first 100 bytes
      ->head file1 file2	>Shows first 10 lines of multiple files
      ->head -q file1 file2	>Suppresses file headers

->tail-The tail command displays the last few lines of a file. By default, it shows the last 10 lines,

         ->tail file.txt	        >Shows last 10 lines of a file
         ->tail -n 5 file.txt	        >Shows last 5 lines
         ->tail -c 100 file.txt	        >Shows last 100 bytes
         ->tail -f file.txt	        >Follows file updates in real-time
         ->tail file1 file2	        >Shows last lines of multiple files
         ->tail -q file1 file2	        >Suppresses file headers

----------------------------------------Fill permission---------------------------------------

->chmod-(change mod)-It modifies the permissions of a file or directory,Read it, Write (modify) it, Execute (run) it.
         
           
Value	Binary	Owner Permissions	Meaning

0	000	---	                No permissions
1	001	--x	                Execute only
2	010	-w-	                Write only
3	011	-wx	                Write and execute
4	100	r--	                Read only
5	101	r-x	                Read and execute
6	110	rw-	                Read and write
7	111	rwx	                Full access: read, write, execute


->chmod 755 file.txt	>Sets read/write/execute for owner, read/execute for others
->chmod u+x file.txt	>Adds execute permission for owner
->chmod g-w file.txt	>Removes write permission from group
->chmod -R 644 folder/	>Recursively sets permissions for all files


->chown-(change owner)-a powerful tool in Unix-like systems (Linux, macOS) used to change the ownership of files and directories.

    ->chown alice file.txt	Changes owner to alice
    ->chown :staff file.txt	Changes group to staff
    ->chown alice:staff file.txt	Changes both owner and group
    ->chown -R bob:dev /project/	Recursively changes ownership in a directory


---------------------------------------Process management-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

->ps-(process status)-it shows you the list of all the programs which are running in your computer.

        ->ps aux   >a=show processes for all users, not just you
                   >u=show the user who started the process
                   >x=Show processes not attached to a terminal (background tasks)


USER       PID  %CPU %MEM COMMAND
root         1   0.0  0.1 /sbin/init
alice     1234   1.2  3.5 /usr/bin/python3 script.py



USER	Who started the process
PID	Process ID (a unique number)
%CPU	How much CPU it’s using
%MEM	How much memory it’s using
COMMAND	What program is running


         ->ps	        >Shows your current terminal’s processes
         ->ps aux	>Shows all running processes in detail
         ->ps -ef	>Shows all processes in full format
         ->ps -u alice	>Shows processes started by user "alice"
         ->ps -ef --forest	>Shows all processes in a tree view

->top-It’s one of the most powerful tools in Unix-like systems (Linux, macOS) for monitoring system activity in real time.

          ->top	         >Shows live system activity
          ->top -u alice >Shows Alice’s processes only
          ->top -d 5	 >Refreshes every 5 seconds
          ->top -p 1234	 >Monitors process with ID 1234


->kill-It tells the computer to end a process.

    ->kill 1234	Sends a default signal to stop process 1234
    ->kill -9 1234	Forcefully kills process 1234 (no mercy!)
    ->kill -15 1234	Politely asks process 1234 to stop

      -9 is like saying “Stop right now!” 
      -15 is like saying “Please stop when you’re ready.”

->killall-Stop the process in a single command
     
       ->killall firefox	>Stops all Firefox processes
       ->killall -i firefox	>Asks before stopping each one
       ->killall -u alice	>Stops all processes started by user "alice"
       ->killall -v firefox	>Shows what’s being stopped


->whoami-whoami is a simple command in Unix-like systems (Linux, macOS) that tells you which user account you’re currently using in the terminal.

->id- >it displays user id and the group id
      >and also check check the another user id and the group id.

->passwd-to change the password of a user account.


        ->passwd	        >Change current user’s password
        ->sudo passwd bob	>Change another user’s password (admin)
        ->passwd -l bob	        >Lock Bob’s account
        ->passwd -u bob	        >Unlock Bob’s account


->sudo-Execute commands as another user (usually root).
Example: sudo apt update

--------------------------------------------------------------------other important commands--------------------------------------------------------------

->history-displays a list of previously executed commands in the current shell session.
      
         history -c	Clears the current session’s history
         history -w     writes the current history to the histpry file
         history -r     reads history from the history file

->clear >clear the terminal screen 
        >removing all previous output and giving you a fresh, empty view.


->echo-displays the text or variables in the terminal.

     ->echo "Hello"	>Prints "Hello"
     ->echo -n "Hi"	>Prints "Hi" without newline
     ->echo -e "A\nB"	>Prints A and B on separate lines
     ->echo $USER	>Prints the current username (variable)
     ->echo "Done" > file.txt	>Writes "Done" to a file


->date-shows the date of the system

     ->date	                >Shows current date and time
     ->date +"%Y-%m-%d"	        >Custom format: Year-Month-Day
     ->date +"%A %H:%M"	        >Day of week and time
     ->sudo date 101016492025	>Sets system date/time (admin only)


->man-man stands for manual. It displays the manual page (or man page) for a given command, utility, or system function. These pages contain detailed documentation including:

What the command does

How to use it

Available options and flags

Examples

